Collatz Conjecture on the Cayley Line — Deep Analysis
opus-2026-03-16

THE REFORMULATION:
  Each Collatz cycle (odd step + halvings) is a STEP on the Cayley line.
  Step size: Δ = (ln(3) - v·ln(2))/2 nats
  where v = v₂(3n+1) = number of trailing zeros of 3n+1.

  For v=1: Δ = ln(3/2)/2 = +0.2027 nats (UP — the fifth doesn't resolve)
  For v=2: Δ = ln(3/4)/2 = -0.1438 nats (DOWN — the fifth resolves)
  For v=3: Δ = ln(3/8)/2 = -0.4904 nats (DOWN — strongly)

  For random odd n: P(v=k) = 1/2^k (geometric distribution).
  P(v=1) = 1/2, P(v≥2) = 1/2. Wait, let me recalculate:
  Actually for 3n+1 where n is odd: 3n+1 is even, so v ≥ 1 always.
  The distribution of v for random odd n:
    P(v=1) = 1/2 (3n+1 ≡ 2 mod 4)
    P(v=2) = 1/4
    P(v=3) = 1/8
    P(v≥k) = 1/2^{k-1}

  E[v] = Σ k/2^k = 2.
  E[Δ] = (ln(3) - E[v]·ln(2))/2 = (ln(3) - 2·ln(2))/2 = ln(3/4)/2 ≈ -0.144 nats.

  Since E[Δ] < 0, the walk has NEGATIVE DRIFT → transient → reaches 0.

  CAVEAT: The drift analysis assumes v₂(3n+1) is independent of the
  trajectory history. This is the HEURISTIC that has not been proved.
  The Terras conjecture (proved for density-1 sets) makes this rigorous
  for "almost all" starting values.

STOPPING TIME PREDICTION:
  If average drift is -0.144 nats/cycle, and distance to 0 is ln(n)/2:
  Expected cycles ≈ ln(n)/(2·0.144) = ln(n)/0.288 ≈ 3.47·ln(n).
  Actual stopping times are 7-12x this (excursions above the start dominate).

MUSICAL INTERPRETATION:
  Each Collatz "up" step adds half a PERFECT FIFTH (ln(3/2)/2) on the
  Cayley line when only one halving occurs.
  Each additional halving removes half an OCTAVE (ln(2)/2).
  "The fifth resolves when you halve twice."
  Collatz conjecture = "the perfect fifth always resolves eventually."

CONNECTION TO log₂(3):
  The critical ratio ln(3)/ln(2) = log₂(3) ≈ 1.585 controls everything:
  - Average halvings per tripling must exceed log₂(3) for drift < 0
  - Heuristic average halvings = 2 > 1.585 ✓
  - The gap 2 - 1.585 = 0.415 controls the drift rate
  - Drift = (1 - 0.415·ln(2))/2 = ... (needs careful calculation)

  The Collatz conjecture is about whether E[v] > log₂(3) for the
  ACTUAL trajectory (not just for random numbers).
